INT. HOSPITAL LAUNDRY ROOM - NIGHT

Industrial washers thump in the dark. Steam. A folding table piled with scrub tops.

DR. ISOLDE NWOSU (40s, surgical cap, tired eyes) sits on an upturned basket with a paper cup of coffee. NURSE BEAUMONT (50s, reading glasses) folds towels. KEOLA (20s, orderly, earbuds around his neck) feeds a load into a dryer. Across the table, DR. MARCHETTI (60s, bow tie, chief of surgery) peels an orange.

BEAUMONT
You could go home, Doctor.

ISOLDE
I could. The couch there is cold.

MARCHETTI
Cold is a good thing in a couch. Keeps one from sleeping through the pager.

KEOLA
Wait. Dr. Marchetti, you actually sleep on a couch?

MARCHETTI
Twenty-six years. I've got a recliner at the hospital and a bed at home I haven't seen since March.

ISOLDE
That's grim.

MARCHETTI
It's tidy. A bed gets mussed. A recliner just waits.

BEAUMONT
He's lying. His wife bought him a very nice bed.

MARCHETTI
My wife is a tyrant who loves me. Keola, is the dryer on four done?

KEOLA
Eleven more minutes.

ISOLDE
Wonderful. Eleven more minutes of this.

The door swings in. ZHANG (30s, pharmacist's coat, a clipboard with a tied-on pen) enters, scanning the room.

ZHANG
Sorry. I'm looking for Dr. Nwosu. Bed twelve needs a sign-off on the morphine.

ISOLDE
That's me.

She drains her coffee, takes the clipboard, signs. Zhang nods and leaves. The door sighs shut.

MARCHETTI
Quiet night.

BEAUMONT
Don't say that.
